# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1
h1("1. Introduction")
p("Network intrusion detection systems (NIDSs) monitor traffic to identify activity that threatens the confidentiality, integrity or availability of networked systems {khraisat}. Signature-based detectors are precise for known attacks but cannot recognise novel ones, which has motivated two decades of research on machine-learning (ML) and deep-learning (DL) detectors that learn the boundary between benign and malicious flows from labelled data {buczak,yin}. The growth of Internet of Things (IoT) and edge deployments, where heterogeneous devices generate large volumes of telemetry and network flows, has made such data-driven monitoring an increasingly important component of sensor-network and IoT security {moustafa_ton,booij}.")
p("Two observations temper the optimism around learned NIDSs. First, operational value is determined less by accuracy than by the false-alarm rate: because benign traffic vastly outnumbers attacks, even a detector with a low false-positive rate (FPR) can bury analysts in alerts {axelsson,sommer}. Second, many reported results do not survive careful scrutiny. Recurrent pitfalls include data snooping, inappropriate baselines, single-run evaluation and spurious correlations that let a model exploit artefacts of the data-collection process rather than attack behaviour {arp,apruzzese}. Benchmark datasets themselves contain duplicated records, labelling problems and distributional differences between their predefined partitions {engelen,zoghi_vis,zoghi_cpe}, and performance measured on one distribution can degrade sharply on another {pendlebury}. Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is frequently proposed as a remedy, yet explanation methods are themselves fallible: different methods disagree, explanations can be unstable, and their faithfulness to the model must be measured rather than assumed {warnecke,krishna,alvarez,maseno}.")
p("Transformers {vaswani} are an attractive architecture for flow-level NIDSs because self-attention can model interactions between heterogeneous flow attributes. In the feature-token formulation popularised for tabular data by the FT-Transformer {gorishniy}, each feature is embedded as a separate token so that attention operates across the features of a single record. A growing number of Transformer-based NIDSs report very high detection rates on UNSW-NB15, TON_IoT, CIC-IDS2017 and related benchmarks {wu_rtids,ullah,umer,zhang2025}. At the same time, tabular-learning research shows that gradient-boosted decision trees remain difficult to beat on typical tabular data {grinsztajn,shwartz}. Whether attention-based models improve flow-level detection, at which operating point they should be deployed, and whether their explanations can be trusted are therefore empirical questions that require a protocol strict enough to answer them.")
p("This paper extends the author's MSc project on a SHAP-explained feature-token Transformer for UNSW-NB15. It does not propose a new architecture. The model studied here, denoted FTT-IDS, is a compact member of the feature-token family, and the contribution is an empirical one. Three questions are asked that a practitioner would raise before deploying such a model. (i) Under a leakage-controlled, multi-seed protocol, how does a feature-token Transformer compare with tuned tree-based and neural baselines, both at fixed thresholds and at matched false-alarm rates? (ii) Can false alarms be controlled using only information available before testing, how well does a false-alarm budget set on validation data transfer to test data from a shifted distribution or from unseen hosts, and what is needed to restore it? (iii) Are the SHAP explanations of the model faithful and stable enough to support analyst decisions, and what do they reveal about its errors? The contributions are as follows:")
bullet("**A leakage-controlled comparison** of a feature-token Transformer with random forest (RF), XGBoost, multilayer perceptron (MLP), one-dimensional convolutional network (1D-CNN) and FT-Transformer baselines on UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT, with exact-duplicate removal before splitting, validation-only selection, five seeds, paired tests, a matched-FPR comparison and controls for training and test duplicates. The Transformer is competitive with, but not better than, gradient-boosted trees.")
bullet("**An analysis of validation-based decision rules.** A threshold fixed at a validation false-alarm budget removes about 80% of the false alarms of every model on UNSW-NB15, whereas F1-optimal and Youden thresholds do not. A sweep of the budget shows that the realised test FPR of the Transformer is three to five times the validation budget at every level, and that the default threshold of 0.5 is itself an implicit budget of 8–10% validation FPR.")
bullet("**A host-disjoint evaluation of TON_IoT.** When the test set consists of source hosts that were not seen in training, the near-perfect random-split results do not hold: the test FPR of all five models rises to between 0.5% and 54% depending on the held-out hosts, although validation data continue to indicate an FPR of about 1%.")
bullet("**A quantification of budget transfer under shift and a remedy with a stated limit.** On UNSW-NB15 the overshoot is decomposed into a change in the mixture of benign sub-populations and a change within them. On both datasets, recalibrating the threshold on about 1000 benign flows from the target network restores the 1% budget for every model without attack labels; the recall that remains at this budget ranges from nearly 100% to below 10% and shows how much of the detector transfers.")
bullet("**An explanation-reliability audit** with two attribution methods (expected-gradients SHAP and Integrated Gradients) that measures faithfulness with and without retraining, stability across backgrounds and retrained models, and agreement with permutation importance, and that uses outcome-wise attribution profiles and feature-removal experiments to show that time-to-live (TTL) features are proxies for the train–test shift rather than its cause.")
p("The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 describes the datasets, protocol, models, decision rules and explanation analysis. Section 4 reports the results, Section 5 discusses their implications and limitations, and Section 6 concludes.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2
h1("2. Related Work")
h2("2.1. Benchmark Datasets and Evaluation Pitfalls")
p("UNSW-NB15 was generated with the IXIA PerfectStorm tool to combine contemporary benign traffic with nine attack families, and it ships with predefined training (175,341 records) and testing (82,332 records) partitions {moustafa2015,moustafa2016}. TON_IoT extends this line of work to IoT and IIoT testbeds with telemetry, operating-system and network data, the latter described by Zeek-derived flow features {moustafa_ton,booij}. Newer IoT datasets such as Edge-IIoTset and CICIoT2023 follow similar designs {ferrag,neto}. Engelen et al. showed that labelling and feature-extraction errors in CIC-IDS2017 substantially alter reported performance {engelen}, and Zoghi and Serpen documented class overlap, imbalance and record duplication in UNSW-NB15 {zoghi_vis,zoghi_cpe}. Sarhan et al. demonstrated that feature sets that differ across datasets hamper the generalisation and comparability of NIDS models {sarhan}. More broadly, Arp et al. catalogued ten pitfalls in ML-based security research, including sampling bias, data snooping, spurious correlations, inappropriate baselines and inappropriate performance measures {arp}; Pendlebury et al. showed that evaluations which ignore distributional differences between training and deployment data overestimate performance {pendlebury}; and Apruzzese et al. argued that NIDS evaluations must reflect operational constraints such as false-alarm budgets {apruzzese}. These concerns echo the earlier warnings of Sommer and Paxson {sommer} and Axelsson's base-rate analysis {axelsson}. The present study adopts these recommendations as explicit protocol choices and quantifies their effect.")
h2("2.2. Transformers for Tabular and Flow-Based Intrusion Detection")
p("Self-attention {vaswani} has been adapted to tabular data either by contextualising categorical embeddings only, as in TabTransformer {huang}, or by tokenising every feature, as in FT-Transformer {gorishniy}. In intrusion detection, RTIDS combined positional embeddings with stacked encoder–decoder layers for CIC-IDS2017 and CIC-DDoS2019 {wu_rtids}; IDS-INT combined Transformer-based transfer learning with SMOTE, CNN and LSTM components and added XAI {ullah}; Umer et al. paired wrapper-based feature selection with a multi-head attention Transformer on UNSW-NB15 {umer}; and Zhang et al. fused a Transformer with a CNN-BiLSTM for IoT intrusion detection {zhang2025}. These studies report results at the default decision threshold, and most report a single run. Benchmarks of tabular learning find that tree ensembles such as random forests {breiman} and XGBoost {chen_xgb} remain at least as accurate as deep models on medium-sized tabular datasets {grinsztajn,shwartz}. A fair assessment of attention-based NIDSs therefore requires tuned tree-based baselines, repeated runs and an explicit treatment of the operating point.")
h2("2.3. Explainable Intrusion Detection and Explanation Reliability")
p("SHAP attributes a prediction to input features through Shapley values {lundberg}, and its gradient-based variant uses expected gradients to explain differentiable models efficiently {erion}; Integrated Gradients attributes a prediction by integrating gradients along a path from a baseline input {sundararajan}. XAI-based NIDSs commonly use SHAP or LIME to rank features globally or to explain individual alerts {neupane,sharma}. Explanations, however, can be unfaithful or unstable. Warnecke et al. proposed descriptive accuracy, sparsity, stability and efficiency criteria for explanations in security applications {warnecke}; Hooker et al. showed that feature-removal benchmarks are needed to verify that attributions identify genuinely important inputs {hooker}; Alvarez-Melis and Jaakkola reported that small input perturbations can change explanations {alvarez}; and Krishna et al. documented systematic disagreement between explanation methods {krishna}. Maseno et al. audited the reliability of SHAP explanations for tree-based and linear NIDSs on UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT using consistency, faithfulness and retraining-stability metrics {maseno}. The present work complements that audit by examining a deep feature-token Transformer, by measuring stability across independently trained models, and by linking explanations to the model's false alarms. This connects XAI to the literature on shortcut learning, in which models exploit spurious cues that do not generalise {geirhos,lapuschkin}, and on dataset shift {moreno}.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3
h1("3. Materials and Methods")
p("Figure 1 summarises the study design. All code, configurations and per-run predictions are released (see the Data Availability Statement), and every experiment can be reproduced from a single entry-point script.")
fig("fig1_framework.png", "Overview of the evaluation framework. Stage I prepares leakage-controlled data; Stage II trains the feature-token Transformer, its ablations and five baselines with five seeds; Stage III compares decision rules, sweeps the false-alarm budget and recalibrates it under shift and on unseen hosts; Stage IV audits the SHAP explanations.")
h2("3.1. Datasets")
p("**UNSW-NB15.** The official training and testing files were used {moustafa2015}. Each record has 42 features after removing the record identifier, the attack-category column and the binary label: 39 numerical features and three categorical features (*proto*, *service* and *state*). The task is binary classification of normal (label 0) versus attack (label 1) flows. The official test file was kept unchanged so that results remain comparable with the literature; it contains 37,000 normal and 45,332 attack records (55.1% attacks).")
p("**TON_IoT.** The *Train_Test_Network* file of the TON_IoT network dataset was used {moustafa_ton}. It contains 211,043 records (50,000 normal and 161,043 attacks across nine attack types). Source and destination IP addresses and ports were removed from the feature set because they identify testbed hosts rather than behaviour, as were five high-cardinality free-text fields (*dns_query*, *ssl_subject*, *ssl_issuer*, *http_uri* and *http_user_agent*). The remaining 33 features comprise 14 numerical and 19 categorical features. Because TON_IoT has no predefined test partition, two evaluation designs were used. In the *random split*, which is the design used in most studies of this dataset, the de-duplicated data were split 60/20/20 into training, validation and test sets, stratified by attack type. In the *host-disjoint* design, the 50 source hosts (source IP addresses) of the de-duplicated data were partitioned into five groups with a stratified group five-fold split (split seed 42); each group served once as the test set, and the flows of the remaining hosts were split 80/20 into training and validation sets, stratified by attack type. The source address was used only to form the groups. In this design, validation data come from the training hosts, as they would in practice, whereas the test data come from hosts that the model has never observed.")
h2("3.2. Leakage-Controlled Data Protocol")
p("Exact duplicates (identical feature values, label and attack category) were removed from the UNSW-NB15 training file and from the TON_IoT file **before** any split. Without this step, copies of the same record can fall on both sides of the training–validation split, which inflates validation scores and biases model and threshold selection. Table 1 lists the resulting partitions. In UNSW-NB15, 67,601 records (38.6%) of the training file were duplicates, mostly attacks; removing them changed the attack share of the training data from 68.1% to 51.8%. In TON_IoT, 117,367 records (55.6%) were duplicates once host identifiers had been removed. The UNSW-NB15 training file was then split 80/20 into training and validation sets, stratified by label, with a fixed split seed of 42. The same split was used for every model and every training seed. As a control, four models were also trained on the official training file without duplicate removal (Section 4.6).")
table("Table 1. Data partitions after de-duplication. Percentages give the share of attack records.",
      ["Dataset", "Partition", "Records", "Normal", "Attack", "Attack (%)", "Use"],
      [["UNSW-NB15", "Training file (raw)", "175,341", "56,000", "119,341", "68.1", "Source"],
       ["", "Exact duplicates removed", "67,601", "—", "—", "—", "—"],
       ["", "Training", "86,192", "41,512", "44,680", "51.8", "Fitting"],
       ["", "Validation", "21,548", "10,378", "11,170", "51.8", "Selection, threshold"],
       ["", "Official test", "82,332", "37,000", "45,332", "55.1", "Final evaluation"],
       ["TON_IoT", "Network file (raw)", "211,043", "50,000", "161,043", "76.3", "Source"],
       ["", "Exact duplicates removed", "117,367", "—", "—", "—", "—"],
       ["", "Training", "56,205", "14,202", "42,003", "74.7", "Fitting"],
       ["", "Validation", "18,735", "4,734", "14,001", "74.7", "Selection, threshold"],
       ["", "Test", "18,736", "4,735", "14,001", "74.7", "Final evaluation"]],
      [1200, 1900, 1000, 900, 900, 900, 1600])
p("Preprocessing statistics were estimated on the training partition only and then applied unchanged to the validation and test partitions. Numerical features were transformed with a signed logarithm, sign(*x*)·log(1+|*x*|), to compress heavy-tailed byte, packet and duration counts, and then standardised. Categorical values were mapped to integer indices in alphabetical order, with index 0 reserved for categories not seen during training (five UNSW-NB15 test records have an unseen *state* value). Models that consume a single numerical vector (the 1D-CNN, the single-token Transformer and the ablations with ordinal categoricals) receive the index minus one, standardised, as in the original MSc implementation. Tree models receive the raw numerical values and the ordinal codes, because they are invariant to monotone transformations.")
p("The UNSW-NB15 test file also contains duplicates (26,387 records, 32.0%), and 8,541 test records have a feature vector that also occurs in the training file. Duplicates in a test set do not leak information into training, but they weight the evaluation towards easily recognised attacks. The main results are therefore reported on the official test set, and Section 4.6 repeats the evaluation on a de-duplicated test set (55,945 records) and on a de-duplicated test set without training overlap (53,915 records).")

h2("3.3. Feature-Token Transformer")
p("Figure 2 shows the feature-token Transformer evaluated in this study, denoted FTT-IDS. Let a flow record consist of *m* numerical features *x*_{1}, …, *x*_{m} and *k* categorical features *c*_{1}, …, *c*_{k}. Each numerical feature is mapped to a *d*-dimensional token by a feature-specific affine map, and each categorical feature by a feature-specific embedding table:")
eq("t_{i} = x_{i} w_{i} + b_{i},   i = 1, …, m;      t_{m+j} = E_{j}[c_{j}],   j = 1, …, k,", 1)
p("where *w*_{i}, *b*_{i} ∈ ℝ^{d} and *E*_{j} ∈ ℝ^{(|C_{j}|+1)×d}. Because *b*_{i} differs for every feature, it also acts as a learned feature-identity embedding. The token matrix *T* ∈ ℝ^{(m+k)×d} is processed by an *L*-layer Transformer encoder with multi-head self-attention {vaswani}:")
eq("Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^{⊤} / √d_{h}) V,   with Q = TW_{Q}, K = TW_{K}, V = TW_{V},", 2)
p("followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, residual connections and layer normalisation. The encoder outputs are mean-pooled over the *m*+*k* tokens and passed to a linear layer that produces two logits, *o*_{normal} and *o*_{attack}; the attack probability is *p* = softmax(*o*)_{attack}. FTT-IDS uses *d* = 64, *L* = 2, four attention heads, a feed-forward width of 2048, dropout 0.1 and post-normalisation. The tokeniser is that of FT-Transformer {gorishniy}; FTT-IDS differs from it in its smaller width and depth, post-normalisation, standard feed-forward blocks and mean pooling instead of a CLS token. These settings are those of the original MSc model, so that the study isolates the effect of the protocol and of the embedding design rather than of capacity tuning. FTT-IDS is therefore one instance of the feature-token family and is not claimed to be a new architecture.")
fig("fig2_architecture.png", "Architecture of the feature-token Transformer (FTT-IDS). Every numerical feature is mapped by its own affine map and every categorical feature by its own embedding table, so each flow becomes a sequence of *m*+*k* tokens processed by self-attention.")
p("The original MSc model differs in two respects: it uses a single affine map Linear(1→*d*) shared by all features, plus an explicit learnable feature-identity embedding added to each token, and it feeds categorical features as standardised ordinal numbers. Four ablations separate these choices (Table 2): (A1) the MSc model, with shared embedding, identity embedding and ordinal categoricals; (A2) A1 without the identity embedding; (A3) per-feature embedding with ordinal categoricals; and (A4) shared embedding with categorical embedding tables. A2 is a sanity check rather than a competitive design: with a shared map, no identity embedding and mean pooling, the network is invariant to permutations of the features and can only use the multiset of feature values. A further control, (A5) a single-token Transformer, projects the whole feature vector into one token, as in the first experiment of the MSc project. With a sequence of length one, self-attention reduces to an identity mapping, so A5 tests whether attention across feature tokens is useful at all.")

h2("3.4. Baselines and Training")
p("Five baselines cover tree ensembles, generic neural networks and a published tabular Transformer. **RF** {breiman} and **XGBoost** {chen_xgb} represent tree ensembles. The **MLP** has three hidden layers (256, 256, 128) with batch normalisation, ReLU and dropout, and it receives categorical features through 8-dimensional embeddings. The **1D-CNN** applies two convolutional layers (32 and 64 channels, kernel size 3) with batch normalisation, max pooling and a 128-unit dense layer to the standardised feature vector. **FT-Transformer** {gorishniy} follows the authors' default configuration (*d* = 192, three pre-norm layers, eight heads, ReGLU feed-forward blocks, attention dropout 0.2, CLS-token readout, AdamW). TabTransformer {huang} was not included because it contextualises only categorical features, which make up 3 of the 42 UNSW-NB15 features; a recurrent baseline was not included because flow features have no natural order.")
p("All neural models were trained with cross-entropy loss, batch size 256, at most 60 epochs and early stopping with patience 10 on validation loss; the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss was retained. Adam {kingma} was used for all neural models except FT-Transformer, which used AdamW {loshchilov} with weight decay 10^{−5}. Each model received a small hyperparameter grid of comparable size, evaluated on the validation set with training seed 42 and selected by validation ROC-AUC (Table 2). The ablations inherited the learning rate selected for the MSc model, so that only the architectural factor changes. To check that these small grids did not disadvantage either model family, a random search over 20 configurations was additionally run for XGBoost (tree depth, minimum child weight, row and column subsampling, L2 penalty, learning rate and split-loss threshold) and for FTT-IDS (learning rate, dropout, width, depth, feed-forward width and weight decay), again with seed 42 and selection by validation ROC-AUC, and the selected configurations were trained with five seeds. In the host-disjoint TON_IoT experiments, the hyperparameters selected on the random split were reused and three seeds (42–44) were run per host group. Each final configuration was then trained with five seeds (42–46), which change parameter initialisation and mini-batch order but not the data split. Experiments ran in PyTorch {paszke} 2.11 with CUDA 12.8 on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Laptop GPU (8 GB); scikit-learn {pedregosa} 1.9, XGBoost 3.4 and SHAP 0.52 were used for the tree models and explanations.")
table("Table 2. Models, tuning grids (validation set, seed 42) and selected values for UNSW-NB15 / TON_IoT.",
      ["Model", "Description", "Grid", "Selected"],
      [["FTT-IDS", "Per-feature numerical + categorical embeddings, d = 64, 2 layers, 4 heads", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}, 2·10^{−4}}", "5·10^{−4} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["A1: MSc model", "Shared Linear(1→d) + identity embedding, ordinal categoricals", "same as above", "10^{−3} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["A2", "A1 without identity embedding (permutation-invariant)", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A3", "Per-feature embedding, ordinal categoricals", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A4", "Shared embedding + identity + categorical embeddings", "lr from A1", "—"],
       ["A5: single token", "All features → one token (attention is an identity map)", "same as FTT-IDS", "5·10^{−4} / 2·10^{−4}"],
       ["RF", "300 trees", "min_samples_leaf ∈ {1, 5}; max_features ∈ {√p, 0.5}", "(5, 0.5) / (1, √p)"],
       ["XGBoost", "hist, lr 0.05, ≤3000 trees, early stopping 100", "max_depth ∈ {6, 8, 10}", "8 / 10"],
       ["MLP", "256-256-128, BN, ReLU", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}}; dropout ∈ {0.1, 0.3}", "(10^{−3}, 0.1) / (5·10^{−4}, 0.3)"],
       ["1D-CNN", "Conv(32)-Conv(64)-MaxPool-FC(128)", "lr ∈ {10^{−3}, 5·10^{−4}, 2·10^{−4}}", "10^{−3} / 10^{−3}"],
       ["FT-Transformer", "Default configuration of {gorishniy}", "lr ∈ {3·10^{−4}, 10^{−4}}", "10^{−4} / 10^{−4}"]],
      [1500, 3300, 2600, 1800])

h2("3.5. Decision Rules")
p("Every model outputs an attack probability *p*(*x*), and an alert is raised when *p*(*x*) ≥ τ. Four rules for choosing τ were compared, all of which use only validation data: the conventional τ = 0.5; τ_{F1}, which maximises validation F1; τ_{J}, which maximises Youden's index (TPR − FPR) on the validation ROC curve; and a false-alarm budget rule,")
eq("τ_{FPR}(α) = min { τ : FPR_{val}(τ) ≤ α },", 3)
p("which selects the most sensitive threshold whose validation false-positive rate does not exceed a budget α. The rule formalises how a security operations team would configure a detector: the tolerable alert volume is fixed first, and detection is then maximised within that budget {axelsson,apruzzese}. It is the Neyman–Pearson operating point estimated on validation data and is not new in itself; the question examined here is how it behaves, relative to the common alternatives, when the test distribution differs from the validation distribution. The main experiments use α = 1%, fixed in advance for all models and datasets. To show how the conclusions depend on this choice, the budget was additionally swept over α ∈ {0.1, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10}% without retraining.")
p("**Benign-only recalibration.** A budget set on validation data is honoured at test time only if benign validation and benign test traffic produce the same score distribution. To measure what is needed when they do not, the threshold was re-estimated from *n* benign flows of the target distribution: *n* normal records were drawn at random from the official test set, the threshold was placed so that ⌊α*n*⌋ of them score above it, and the model was evaluated on the remaining test records, which exclude the *n* calibration flows. This simulates an operator who records a short period of attack-free traffic on the monitored network; it uses no attack labels and no retraining. The procedure was repeated for *n* ∈ {200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000}, 20 random draws and all five seeds on UNSW-NB15, and with *n* = 1000 on every host group of the host-disjoint TON_IoT design. Because the calibration flows come from the test distribution, these results are reported separately from the validation-only results and are not used for any model comparison.")

h2("3.6. Evaluation and Statistical Analysis")
p("Test performance is reported as accuracy, precision, recall (detection rate), F1, FPR, Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC), ROC-AUC {fawcett} and average precision (AP) {saito}, as mean ± standard deviation over the five seeds. Detection rates per attack category are reported for UNSW-NB15. Because a validation threshold yields a different realised test FPR for each model, threshold-based metrics confound ranking quality with the position of the operating point. Models were therefore also compared at matched test false-alarm rates, using the detection rate at 1% and 5% test FPR and the standardised partial ROC-AUC for FPR ≤ 10% {mcclish}. These quantities are read from the test ROC curve; they describe ranking quality in the operating region of interest and involve no threshold selection. Probability calibration on validation and test data was summarised by the expected calibration error (ECE, 15 equal-width bins) {guo}.")
p("Paired differences between FTT-IDS and each comparator were tested in two ways. McNemar's test {mcnemar,dietterich} was applied to the predictions of each seed pair; the median *p*-value and the number of seeds with *p* < 0.05 are reported. In addition, a paired bootstrap over test records {efron} with 1000 replicates was used: each replicate averages the metric over the five seeds of both models and yields a 95% confidence interval for the difference in F1. The interval reflects sampling of test records with the trained models held fixed; variation between seeds is shown by the standard deviations. No correction for multiple comparisons was applied, so individual *p*-values close to 0.05 should be read as descriptive. A Wilcoxon test over seeds was not used, because with five seeds its smallest attainable two-sided *p*-value is 0.0625.")

h2("3.7. Explanation Analysis")
p("Explanations target the attack margin *f*(*x*) = *o*_{attack}(*x*) − *o*_{normal}(*x*), so that positive SHAP values push a flow towards an alert. SHAP values were computed with GradientExplainer, an implementation of expected gradients {lundberg,erion}, using 500 training records as background and 2000 test records as explanation set (both stratified by label, identical for all seeds) and 500 samples per explanation. For categorical features, attributions were computed in the embedding space and summed over embedding dimensions, so that each original feature receives one value. The additivity error |E[*f*] + Σ_{i} φ_{i} − *f*(*x*)|, which is non-zero for this sampling-based estimator, is reported as a diagnostic. The global importance of feature *i* is the mean absolute SHAP value over the explanation set, averaged over the five seeds.")
p("Four reliability properties were assessed. (i) **Stability across backgrounds**: the global ranking of one model was recomputed with two further background samples and compared using Spearman's ρ, Kendall's τ and the Jaccard overlap of the top-10 features. (ii) **Stability across retrained models**: the same statistics were computed for all ten pairs of the five seeds. (iii) **Faithfulness**: following feature-removal benchmarks {hooker,warnecke}, the *k* globally most important features were replaced by reference values (training mean for numerical features, training mode for categorical features) on the full test set, for *k* ∈ {1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20}; the drop in ROC-AUC was compared with masking *k* random features (five draws). In this test the models were not retrained, so it measures the dependence of the trained model on the masked features. A local variant masked each explained alert's own top-*k* features. (iv) **Agreement with a model-agnostic method**: permutation importance (decrease in ROC-AUC over three repeats on a stratified 20,000-record test subsample) was compared with the SHAP ranking. (v) **A second attribution method**: Integrated Gradients {sundararajan} was computed for the same records and models, with the mean embedded background record as baseline and 64 integration steps; its completeness error, its agreement with the SHAP ranking and its own stability across seeds were measured, and expected gradients were recomputed with 2000 samples per explanation for two seeds to test the sensitivity of the ranking to the sampling error. (vi) **Faithfulness under retraining**: because masking presents the model with inputs outside its training distribution, FTT-IDS and XGBoost were also retrained (seeds 42–44) after deleting the three, five or ten globally top-ranked features from the data, and after deleting two random sets of five and of ten features drawn once in advance, following the remove-and-retrain protocol of Hooker et al. {hooker}. Finally, for error analysis, test records were grouped into true positives (TP), true negatives (TN), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN) at the validation-F1 threshold, which lies close to 0.5 and therefore retains the full population of false alarms, and the mean SHAP profile of up to 400 records per group was computed.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4
