h2("4.2. Ablation Study")
p("Table 7 isolates the design choices of the feature-token Transformer. Two controls behave as expected. The permutation-invariant variant A2, which cannot tell which feature a token represents, loses 3.3 F1 points on UNSW-NB15 (86.25% vs. 89.53% for A1), lowers ROC-AUC from 0.984 to 0.954 and raises the FPR to 34.5%; on TON_IoT its FPR nearly triples (4.71% vs. 1.72%). That it still reaches a ROC-AUC of 0.954 shows how much of the UNSW-NB15 signal is carried by the mere presence of characteristic values. The single-token control A5, in which attention degenerates to an identity map, is the weakest remaining variant on UNSW-NB15 (F1 87.42%, ROC-AUC 0.967; detection rate at 1% test FPR 75.5% versus 85.6% for FTT-IDS, Table 4). On TON_IoT, where the task is nearly separable, A5 performs on par with the feature-token variants. Tokenising features and identifying each token is thus necessary on the harder dataset, which is the expected result and a check on the implementation more than a finding.")
p("The form of the numerical and categorical embedding, by contrast, has no consistent effect. On UNSW-NB15, the four variants with identity information (A1, A3, A4 and FTT-IDS) lie within 0.3 F1 points of each other at τ = 0.5 and within 0.7 points at τ_{FPR}(1%). FTT-IDS is not the best of them: at the budget threshold, A4 attains a higher mean F1 (93.79% vs. 93.65%) and both A1 and A4 a lower FPR (3.75% and 3.77% vs. 4.44%), and A4 has the best ROC-AUC (0.9859). On TON_IoT, which has 19 categorical features, the order is reversed: per-feature and categorical embeddings give a small but significant gain over the MSc model (F1 99.50% vs. 99.40%, ΔROC-AUC +0.0006, McNemar *p* < 0.05 in four of five seeds). The embedding design should therefore be treated as a dataset-dependent hyperparameter. FTT-IDS is retained as the reference configuration in the remaining sections because it is the standard feature-token design {gorishniy}, not because it is the best variant; the MSc model A1 gives the same picture for every analysis that was repeated with it.")
