h2("4.7. Explanation Reliability")
p("**Global explanations.** Figure 7 shows the global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on both datasets. On UNSW-NB15, the explanations are dominated by three TTL-derived features (*ct_state_ttl*, *sttl* and *dttl*), followed by *service* and *proto*. This agrees with the single-seed ranking of the MSc thesis (*sttl*, *ct_state_ttl*, *state*) but is more concentrated on TTL information. On TON_IoT, packet and byte counts (*dst_pkts*, *src_pkts*, *dst_bytes*), the connection state and the duration dominate. The relative additivity error of the expected-gradients estimates was 13–17% on UNSW-NB15 and 13–19% on TON_IoT with 500 samples per explanation. Individual attributions are therefore approximate, and the analysis below relies on aggregates over records and seeds; the effect of the sampling error on the global ranking is examined below.")
fig("fig_global_shap.png", "Global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (left) and TON_IoT (right): mean absolute SHAP value of the attack margin over 2000 test records, averaged over five seeds (error bars: s.d. across seeds).")
p("**Stability.** Table 14 summarises the stability results. For a fixed model, explanations were almost insensitive to the choice of background sample (Spearman ρ ≥ 0.99). Across independently trained models, stability was high on TON_IoT (ρ = 0.95) but only moderate on UNSW-NB15 (ρ = 0.76, Kendall τ = 0.60), and the top-10 feature sets of two seeds had a Jaccard overlap of only 0.57, i.e., about seven common features. The MSc model (A1) behaved similarly (ρ = 0.75). The large error bars in Figure 7 show the same effect: two models with practically identical accuracy can distribute importance differently among correlated TTL features. A single-seed SHAP ranking, as reported in most XAI-NIDS studies, is therefore one draw from a distribution of plausible explanations.")
table("Table 14. Reliability of FTT-IDS explanations (mean ± s.d.). Faithfulness: test ROC-AUC after masking the k most important vs. k random features (no retraining).",
      ["Property", "Statistic", "UNSW-NB15", "TON_IoT"],
      [["Stability across backgrounds", "Spearman ρ / top-10 Jaccard", "0.998 / 0.91", "0.990 / 0.82"],
       ["Stability across 5 seeds (10 pairs)", "Spearman ρ", "0.764 ± 0.062", "0.949 ± 0.015"],
       ["", "Kendall τ", "0.597 ± 0.058", "0.828 ± 0.026"],
       ["", "Top-10 Jaccard", "0.568 ± 0.095", "0.727 ± 0.078"],
       ["Agreement with permutation importance", "Spearman ρ / top-10 overlap", "0.760 / 7 of 10", "0.861 / 8 of 10"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 5", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.738 vs. 0.980", "0.824 vs. 0.971"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 10", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.746 vs. 0.922", "0.648 vs. 0.934"],
       ["Local deletion, k = 5 (seed 42)", "Alert flip rate: SHAP vs. random", "7.5% vs. 2.6%", "0.3% vs. 0.6%"]],
      [2900, 2700, 1850, 1850])
p("**Faithfulness without retraining.** Figure 8 shows that masking the globally most important features degrades the model far more than masking random features. On UNSW-NB15, masking the top five features lowers ROC-AUC from 0.984 to 0.738, while masking five random features leaves it at 0.980; on TON_IoT the corresponding values are 0.824 and 0.971. The global rankings therefore identify features on which the trained model depends. Two qualifications apply. First, the curve is not monotone on UNSW-NB15 (0.738 for *k* = 5 and 0.746 for *k* = 10) and its variance across seeds is large (s.d. 0.09–0.10), so the ranking is informative for the leading features but not for the order of the features below them; the Integrated Gradients ranking examined below does not show this irregularity. Second, the evidence at the level of individual alerts is weak: masking an alert's own top five features flipped 7.5% of UNSW-NB15 alerts (2.6% for random features) and lowered the attack probability by 0.137 (0.015 for random features), and on TON_IoT local deletion had no measurable effect, because alerts are supported by many redundant features. The SHAP values are thus suitable for ranking features globally and for comparing groups of records, but they do not identify a small set of features that is decisive for a single alert. Permutation importance, a model-agnostic method, agreed with SHAP on seven (UNSW-NB15) and eight (TON_IoT) of the top-10 features.")
fig("fig_faithfulness.png", "Faithfulness of global SHAP rankings: test ROC-AUC of FTT-IDS after replacing the k most important features (blue) or k random features (orange) by reference values, without retraining (mean ± s.d. over five seeds; random masks also averaged over five draws).")

p("**A second attribution method and the sampling error.** Table 15 reports the checks with Integrated Gradients (IG) on UNSW-NB15. IG satisfies completeness almost exactly (relative error 1.3–4.0%), and its five leading features are the same five that SHAP ranks first (*ct_state_ttl*, *dttl*, *service*, *sttl* and *proto*). Beyond these, the two methods agree only partly: per model, the Spearman correlation between the IG and SHAP rankings is 0.86–0.93 and the Jaccard overlap of the top-10 sets 0.43–0.82, which is another instance of the disagreement problem {krishna}. IG is no more stable across retrained models than SHAP (Spearman 0.73 ± 0.08, top-10 Jaccard 0.42 ± 0.08), so the instability reported above is a property of the retrained models and not of one attribution method. Masking the IG top-*k* features degrades the model monotonically (ROC-AUC 0.96, 0.93, 0.91, 0.80 and 0.69 for *k* = 1, 2, 3, 5 and 10, against 0.98, 0.97, 0.97, 0.97 and 0.92 for random features). Increasing the number of expected-gradients samples from 500 to 2000 lowers the additivity error from 13–15% to 6–7% for the two models tested and leaves the global ranking unchanged (Spearman 0.998, identical top-10 sets). The sampling error thus affects individual attributions but not the global rankings used in this study.")
table("Table 15. Checks of the FTT-IDS explanations on UNSW-NB15 with a second attribution method (Integrated Gradients, IG; five seeds) and with more expected-gradients (EG) samples (seeds 42 and 43).",
      ["Check", "Statistic", "Value"],
      [["IG completeness", "Relative error, range over seeds", "1.3–4.0%"],
       ["EG additivity, 500 → 2000 samples", "Relative error", "13.2% → 5.9%; 15.4% → 6.9%"],
       ["EG ranking, 500 vs. 2000 samples", "Spearman ρ / top-10 Jaccard", "0.998 / 1.00"],
       ["IG vs. EG ranking, same model", "Spearman ρ / top-10 Jaccard, range", "0.86–0.93 / 0.43–0.82"],
       ["IG stability across 5 seeds (10 pairs)", "Spearman ρ / Kendall τ / top-10 Jaccard", "0.73 ± 0.08 / 0.56 ± 0.06 / 0.42 ± 0.08"],
       ["IG masking faithfulness, k = 5", "AUC: IG top-k vs. random k", "0.804 vs. 0.965"],
       ["IG masking faithfulness, k = 10", "AUC: IG top-k vs. random k", "0.694 vs. 0.921"]],
      [3300, 3300, 3000])
p("**Faithfulness under retraining.** Table 16 shows what happens when features are deleted from the data and the models are retrained. The result differs sharply from the masking test. Without the five top-ranked features, FTT-IDS still reaches a ROC-AUC of 0.980 and XGBoost of 0.981, compared with 0.984 with all features; without the ten top-ranked features, the ROC-AUC is 0.984 and 0.986. Deleting random features has effects of the same size, and one of the two random sets of five features (*proto*, *sbytes*, *sinpkt*, *smean* and *ct_dst_ltm*) costs more than the top five (ROC-AUC 0.975 and 0.981; detection rate at 1% FPR 74.2% and 81.8%, against 80.4% and 84.4% without the top five). Under retraining, the top-ranked features are therefore not consistently more necessary than randomly chosen ones. The two tests answer different questions. Masking shows that the attributions describe what the trained model uses; retraining shows that the model could have used something else, because the UNSW-NB15 features are highly redundant. Global attribution rankings should consequently be read as a description of one model's decision basis, and not as a statement about which measurements a detector needs. This is the general form of the TTL result reported in the next section.")
table("Table 16. Faithfulness under retraining on UNSW-NB15: test ROC-AUC and detection rate at 1% test FPR (%) after deleting features and retraining (mean ± s.d. over seeds 42–44). Top-k sets follow the five-seed SHAP ranking of FTT-IDS; random sets were drawn once in advance.",
      ["Deleted features", "FTT-IDS ROC-AUC", "FTT-IDS TPR at 1% FPR", "XGBoost ROC-AUC", "XGBoost TPR at 1% FPR"],
      [["None", "0.9845 ± 0.0015", "85.63 ± 1.56", "0.9840 ± 0.0002", "86.23 ± 0.29"],
       ["SHAP top 3 (the TTL features)", "0.9820 ± 0.0002", "84.03 ± 0.38", "0.9821 ± 0.0001", "85.46 ± 0.19"],
       ["SHAP top 5", "0.9797 ± 0.0015", "80.43 ± 2.58", "0.9810 ± 0.0003", "84.39 ± 0.10"],
       ["Random 5, set a", "0.9748 ± 0.0028", "74.17 ± 3.21", "0.9806 ± 0.0007", "81.75 ± 0.55"],
       ["Random 5, set b", "0.9849 ± 0.0007", "86.18 ± 0.72", "0.9845 ± 0.0002", "86.73 ± 0.18"],
       ["SHAP top 10", "0.9837 ± 0.0009", "81.74 ± 2.60", "0.9859 ± 0.0002", "86.38 ± 0.25"],
       ["Random 10, set a", "0.9826 ± 0.0011", "83.53 ± 0.91", "0.9830 ± 0.0003", "85.51 ± 0.26"],
       ["Random 10, set b", "0.9844 ± 0.0011", "85.33 ± 1.67", "0.9852 ± 0.0002", "86.27 ± 0.14"]],
      [2600, 1700, 1800, 1700, 1800])

h2("4.8. What Explains the False Alarms?")
p("Figure 9 shows the mean SHAP profile of each prediction outcome on UNSW-NB15. False positives look like true positives in the TTL features: *ct_state_ttl* and *sttl* push false alarms towards the attack class (+2.46 and +2.16) almost as strongly as genuine attacks (+3.25 and +3.01), whereas these features push true negatives firmly towards normal (−2.42 and −2.26). For 56% of the explained false alarms, *sttl* is the single largest contributor. The non-TTL features (for example *service* and *proto*) instead point weakly towards normal in false alarms. The model raises these alerts because of their TTL values. Integrated Gradients gives the same picture from a different reference point: relative to an average training record, *ct_state_ttl* pulls true negatives strongly towards normal (mean attribution between −4.2 and −9.4 across the five models), whereas for false positives and true positives alike its attribution is close to zero (between −0.9 and +0.7). False alarms are benign flows that lack the TTL signature of benign training traffic.")
fig("fig_shap_by_outcome.png", "Mean SHAP value of the ten globally most important features for true positives (TP), false negatives (FN), false positives (FP) and true negatives (TN) of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (seed 42, validation-F1 threshold; up to 400 records per group). Red pushes towards an alert, blue towards normal.")
p("Figure 10 shows the data behind this behaviour. In the de-duplicated training data, 75% of normal flows have *sttl* = 31 and only 19% have *sttl* = 254, whereas 75% of attacks have *sttl* = 254. In the official test set, the share of normal flows with *sttl* = 254 doubles to 41%. Of the false alarms of FTT-IDS at τ = 0.5, 88% are normal flows with *sttl* = 254, most of them with state FIN (73%) and no identified service (83%). At the budget threshold, the share is still 80%.")
fig("fig_sttl_shift.png", "Distribution of the source TTL (sttl) by partition and class in UNSW-NB15 (training data after de-duplication; official test set). Normal test traffic contains a much larger share of sttl = 254 flows than normal training traffic.")
p("A natural remedy would be to remove the TTL features. To test this, FTT-IDS and XGBoost were retrained with five seeds without *sttl* and *ct_state_ttl*, and again without all three TTL features (*sttl*, *dttl* and *ct_state_ttl*). Neither intervention reduced false alarms. Without any TTL feature, FTT-IDS reached F1 89.77% and FPR 24.98% at τ = 0.5 (93.17% and 4.85% at the budget threshold); XGBoost reached 89.29% and 26.45% (93.21% and 5.86%). Moreover, the false alarms of the TTL-free models are largely the *same flows*. The Jaccard overlap between the false-alarm sets of the full and the TTL-free FTT-IDS was 0.77 on average over seeds, and 88% of the TTL-free model's false alarms still have *sttl* = 254, even though the model never saw this feature. The TTL fields are therefore not the cause of the errors but the most legible proxy for a sub-population of benign test traffic that is under-represented among normal training flows. Without them, the model recognises the same population through correlated features. SHAP correctly points to the proxy; feature removal does not fix the underlying shift, in line with the retraining results of Table 16. Interventions on the decision, namely the budget threshold and its recalibration on target traffic (Section 4.4), remain the effective controls.")

h2("4.9. Model Size and Inference Cost")
p("FTT-IDS has 577,410 trainable parameters on UNSW-NB15; most of them belong to the 2048-wide feed-forward blocks inherited from the MSc configuration. FT-Transformer has 937,538 parameters, the MLP 117,858 and the 1D-CNN 178,946. On a laptop CPU, scoring a single flow took a median of 1.43 ms with FTT-IDS, compared with 2.49 ms with FT-Transformer and 0.31 ms with the MLP and the 1D-CNN. On the GPU, FTT-IDS scored about 95,000 flows per second in batches of 1024. No claim of a lightweight design is made. These figures only indicate that per-flow scoring is not a bottleneck for offline or near-real-time analysis on commodity hardware.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5
h1("5. Discussion")
p("**Accuracy is not the differentiator.** Under a protocol that removes duplicate-induced leakage and gives every model a comparable tuning budget, the feature-token Transformer matches but does not beat gradient-boosted trees on UNSW-NB15, at fixed thresholds as well as at matched false-alarm rates, and it trails them slightly on TON_IoT. This result is consistent with tabular-learning benchmarks {grinsztajn,shwartz}. It also suggests that some of the near-perfect accuracies reported for Transformer-based NIDSs may owe more to evaluation choices than to architecture {arp}. Within the feature-token family, the embedding design made no consistent difference, and the configuration carried forward from the MSc thesis performed as well as the standard one. For practitioners, the choice between a Transformer and a tree ensemble can therefore rest on other criteria, such as the availability of gradient-based explanations, integration into end-to-end pipelines or latency.")
p("**Operating points matter more than models, and the default threshold is a hidden choice.** The largest effect in this study comes from the decision rule. Changing the model alters F1 by less than one point; moving from τ = 0.5 to a 1% validation budget removes over 80% of the false alarms of every model and raises F1 by about four points. Thresholding a score at a false-alarm budget is standard practice in detection theory and is not presented here as a new method. What the analysis adds is evidence about how the common rules behave on a widely used benchmark: the default threshold corresponds to a validation false-alarm rate of 8–10%, the F1-optimal and Youden rules inherit this permissiveness, and all three therefore place the detector at an operating point that few analysts would choose deliberately. Reporting results only at τ = 0.5 or as ROC-AUC hides this degree of freedom. NIDS studies should state the decision rule explicitly and report the FPR at a fixed, operationally motivated operating point.")
p("**Validation data say little about unseen conditions.** Under the train–test shift of UNSW-NB15, the realised false-alarm rate was several times the validation budget for every model and every budget (three to five times for FTT-IDS), and most of the excess arose within benign sub-populations, where it cannot be corrected by re-weighting. On TON_IoT the effect was larger and had a concrete cause: with test hosts that were absent from training, the false-alarm rate ranged from the 1% suggested by validation data to more than 50%, driven in each case by a single unfamiliar host. Random splits, which are the norm for TON_IoT and many other benchmarks, place flows of every host on both sides of the split and cannot reveal this. Results on such splits should be read as an upper bound that holds only for the machines the detector was trained on, and host-disjoint or otherwise grouped splits should be reported whenever host identifiers are available.")
p("**Recalibration controls false alarms and exposes what transfers.** A threshold derived from about 1000 benign flows of the target network restored the 1% budget on both datasets, for every model, without attack labels or retraining. This points to a simple deployment recommendation: a detector trained on a benchmark should not be shipped with its training-time threshold; the threshold should be set, and periodically refreshed, from a short window of recent traffic that is believed to be attack-free. Recalibration is not a repair, however. It fixes the false-alarm rate and leaves the ranking of flows untouched, so the recall that remains at the recalibrated threshold, which ranged from nearly 100% to less than 10% across host groups and models, measures how much of the learned detector is valid in the new environment. Where it is low, the model has to be retrained or adapted with data from the target network; no threshold can compensate. In the host-disjoint experiments the random forest retained the most recall, which suggests that robustness to unseen hosts, and not accuracy on a random split, is the property on which model families differ.")
p("**Explanations need their own evaluation.** The global SHAP rankings of FTT-IDS passed the faithfulness and model-agnostic agreement checks, which supports their use for feature-level analysis. They were nevertheless only moderately stable across retrained models on UNSW-NB15 with both attribution methods, a finding that matches the retraining-stability results reported for tree models by Maseno et al. {maseno}; they were weakly faithful at the level of single alerts; and under retraining the top-ranked features proved not consistently more necessary than random ones. Analysts who receive a single-model explanation should not over-interpret the precise ranking of correlated features or treat the top-ranked features of one alert as its cause; aggregating explanations over several models, as done here, gives a more reliable picture. The most useful application of SHAP in this study was diagnostic. Contrasting the explanations of true and false positives revealed that false alarms are driven by TTL values, which in turn exposed a train–test shift in the benchmark. This is the type of shortcut that the XAI literature warns about {geirhos,lapuschkin}. The study also illustrates a limit of explanations: identifying the proxy feature did not make the problem removable at the feature level, because attributions describe the decision basis of one trained model and not the information content of the data.")
p("**Limitations.** First, only two datasets were used, both produced by the same research group and both with flow features; conclusions may differ for packet-level models and for datasets such as CICIoT2023 or Edge-IIoTset {neto,ferrag}. The host-disjoint design covers one testbed with 50 source hosts, a few of which dominate the benign traffic, so the per-group results have high variance by construction and should be read as a demonstration that the problem exists, not as an estimate of its typical size. Second, the study addresses binary detection; the per-category analysis covers multi-class behaviour only indirectly. Third, the hyperparameter grids were deliberately small and equal in size, and the host-disjoint experiments reused the hyperparameters of the random split. A random search over 20 further configurations left the results of XGBoost and FTT-IDS unchanged, but it does not cover the other models. Fourth, the validation set served for early stopping, hyperparameter selection and threshold setting, and seed 42 was used for tuning as well as for one of the reported runs; both introduce a small optimistic bias that affects all models alike. Fifth, the retraining-based faithfulness test used three seeds and two random feature sets per size, which is enough to show that top-ranked features are not uniquely necessary but not to rank feature sets. Finally, the recalibration experiments draw benign flows at random from the test distribution, which is an optimistic stand-in for a recorded attack-free window; contamination of the window with undetected attacks and drift between recalibrations {pendlebury} would both increase the realised error and were not studied.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6
h1("6. Conclusions")
p("This study re-evaluated a SHAP-explained feature-token Transformer for network intrusion detection under a protocol designed to avoid common evaluation pitfalls: duplicate removal before splitting, validation-only model and threshold selection, five seeds with paired tests, comparison at matched false-alarm rates, evaluation on unseen hosts, and an audit of explanation reliability with two attribution methods. On UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT, the Transformer was competitive with, but not superior to, tuned gradient-boosted trees, and the design of its feature embeddings made no consistent difference. The operating point and the evaluation design had far larger effects than the model. On UNSW-NB15, a threshold fixed at a 1% validation false-alarm budget lowered the test false-positive rate of every model by 18–23 points (from 26.6% to 4.4% for the Transformer), whereas the default, F1-optimal and Youden thresholds all operated above 24%; the budget was still exceeded fourfold because of a train–test shift. On TON_IoT, a random split suggested a false-positive rate near 1% for every model, while evaluation on unseen hosts gave between 0.5% and 54%. Recalibrating the threshold on about 1000 benign flows from the target distribution restored the 1% budget in all cases, and the recall that remained (86% on UNSW-NB15; between 7% and 100% for the Transformer on TON_IoT) showed how much of each detector transferred. SHAP and Integrated Gradients agreed on the leading features and were faithful to the trained models, but rankings were only moderately stable across retrained models, weakly faithful for single alerts, and uninformative about which features are necessary: retraining without the top-ranked features, including the TTL features that drive the false alarms, changed little. Future work will extend the protocol to multi-class detection and to further IoT datasets with host or temporal structure, study recalibration under drift and with contaminated benign windows, and examine training methods that improve transfer to unseen hosts.")

